<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Boris Skossyreff</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1989">1989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Boris I of Andorra</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Boris Skossyreff was a Russian émigré adventurer who in July 1934 proclaimed himself King Boris I of Andorra and issued a constitution for the principality. Within days the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Urgell had him arrested by the Spanish Civil Guard, and he was tried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> and expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He spent much of his later life in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, where he died in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/boris-skossyreff.jpg" alt="Boris Skossyreff" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boris_I_Andorra_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
